	shorttitle = {The growth of {H} {II} regions around massive stars},
	url = {http://arxiv.org/abs/2101.01193},
	doi = {10.1093/mnras/staa3992},
	abstract = {Gas metallicity ������ and the related dust-to-gas ratio ������d can inﬂuence the growth of H regions via metal line cooling and UV absorption. We model these eﬀects in star-forming regions containing massive stars. We compute stellar feedback from photoionization and radiation pressure (RP) using Monte Carlo radiative transfer coupled with hydrodynamics, including stellar and diﬀuse radiation ﬁelds. We follow a 105 M turbulent cloud with ������/Z = 2, 1, 0.5, 0.1 and ������d = 0.01������/Z with a cluster-sink particle method for star formation. The models evolve for at least 1.5 Myr under feedback. Lower ������ results in higher temperatures and therefore larger H regions. For ������ Z , radiation pressure ������rad can dominate locally over the gas pressure ������gas in the inner half-parsec around sink particles. Globally, the ratio of ������rad/������gas is around 1 (2 Z ), 0.3 (Z ), 0.1 (0.5 Z ), and 0.03 (0.1 Z ). In the solar model, excluding RP results in an ionized volume several times smaller than the ﬁducial model with both mechanisms. Excluding RP and UV attenuation by dust results in a larger ionized volume than the ﬁducial case. That is, UV absorption hinders growth more than RP helps it. The radial expansion velocity of ionized gas reaches +15 km s−1 outwards, while neutral gas has inward velocities for most of the runtime, except for 0.1 Z which exceeds +4 km s−1. ������ and ������d do not signiﬁcantly alter the star formation eﬃciency, rate, or cluster half-mass radius, with the exception of 0.1 Z due to the earlier expulsion of neutral gas.},
	language = {en},
